@@ -153,6 +153,43 @@ The following directives are provided for module and class contents:
       Describe the location where the object is defined.  The default value is
       the module specified by :rst:dir:`py:currentmodule`.
 
+.. rst:directive:: .. py:type:: name
+
+   Describes a type alias.  Type aliases may be declared at module level or
+   inside a class, and may include a description body.  References to an alias
+   can use the :rst:role:`py:type` role.
+
+   The optional ``canonical`` option describes the type represented by the
+   alias.  When supplied, it appears after the alias name with an equals sign
+   and is parsed as a Python annotation, so referenced types can be linked.
+
+   For example::
+
+      .. py:type:: UserId
+         :canonical: int
+
+         An identifier for a user.
+
+   .. rubric:: options
+
+   .. rst:directive:option:: canonical
+      :type: Python type expression
+
+      Describe the type represented by the alias and show it in the signature.
+      This option is optional.
+
+   .. rst:directive:option:: module
+      :type: text
+
+      Describe the module where the alias is defined.  The default value is
+      the module specified by :rst:dir:`py:currentmodule`.
+
+.. rst:role:: py:type
+
+   Reference a type alias declared with :rst:dir:`py:type`.  As with other
+   Python-domain references, aliases can also be referenced using
+   :rst:role:`py:obj`.
+
 .. rst:directive:: .. py:exception:: name
                    .. py:exception:: name(parameters)
                    .. py:exception:: name[type parameters](parameters)
